From ecbe37a369cc94b44eb396fd89461674fdb7d9cd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 15:55:40 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] QSDK-CP: agent/bwl: fix cross-band vht_center_frequency
 (5530) on 2.4GHz radio and downstream FREQ_TYPE=255 failures

On MLO platforms several links (2.4/5/6 GHz) share a single base netdev and a
shared/global hostapd control socket. Two independent code paths copied a
co-located 5/6 GHz link's center frequency onto a 2.4 GHz radio's
m_radio_info.vht_center_freq without any band check, and one agent path then
assumed a wrong band when the resulting WifiChannel came out empty. The net
effect on a 2.4 GHz radio (phy00.0, channel 6) was a reported
vht_center_frequency=5530 and a cascade of FREQ_UNKNOWN (255) failures.

Observed symptoms (all the same root cause):
  ap_manager.cpp   CSA_Finished: channel = 6 ... vht_center_frequency = 5530
  beerocks_wifi_channel.cpp  WIFI_CHANNEL CREATION ... FREQ_TYPE: 255 (chan 1..11)
  son_wireless_utils.cpp     Unknown freq type 255, must be 2.4GHz/5GHz/6GHz
  son_wireless_utils.cpp     Invalid freq type Unknown Frequency, wifiChannel is empty
  dynamic_channel_selection_r2_task.cpp  All channels set to highest preference
                                         due to empty operating classes list
  dynamic_channel_selection_r2_task.cpp  Preference for radio ... (empty)

Causal chain:
  1. A CTRL-EVENT-CHANNEL-SWITCH / GET_INTERFACE for a 5GHz link is delivered to
     the 2.4GHz radio's HAL over the shared socket; its cf1 (=5530) is stored
     verbatim into vht_center_freq and reported on CSA_Finished/ACS_Completed.
  2. son_slave builds radio->wifi_channel from (channel=6, center=5530);
     which_freq_type(5530)=5G does not match channel 6, so WifiChannel fails
     validation and becomes empty (m_freq_type = FREQ_UNKNOWN).
  3. The backhaul/controller then read radio->wifi_channel.get_freq_type()
     = FREQ_UNKNOWN (255) and pass it into WifiChannel()/which_freq_type(),
     producing the "FREQ_TYPE: 255"/"Unknown freq type 255"/empty-WifiChannel
     logs, op_class=0 for every 2.4GHz (channel,bw), an empty operating-class
     list, and an empty channel-preference set for the 2.4GHz radio.

This is NOT a CBS/CPR defect and NOT a hostapd defect: base hostapd's
vht_oper_centr_freq_seg0_idx is 0 for a plain 2.4GHz AP and the QCA
ctrl_iface_extn.c STATUS never emits it. The wrong value is introduced purely
in the prplMesh nl80211 HAL center-frequency handling; the "255" logs are its
downstream effect.

Fixes:

1) common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
   process_nl80211_event() Event::CTRL_Channel_Switch [authoritative]:
   Band-guard the hostapd-reported cf1 before storing it. Only accept cf1 when
   which_freq_type(cf1) matches this radio's frequency_band (5GL/5GH normalized
   to 5G; FREQ_UNKNOWN radio band accepts as before). For a mismatched
   (cross-band) cf1, fall back to the primary freq for 20MHz (always band-correct
   for a 2.4GHz radio, e.g. 2437 for ch6) and log a WARNING; wider bandwidths
   keep the band-correct value from refresh_radio_info(). This is the path that
   produced the persistent CSA_Finished vht_center_frequency=5530 on 2.4GHz.

2) common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
   refresh_radio_info():
   Band-guard the Priority-1 nl80211 GET_INTERFACE center frequency
   (if_info.frequency_center1) with the same rule, covering the ACS/refresh
   path that also seeds vht_center_freq. Cross-band values are ignored (DEBUG
   log) and the correct center is derived from channel_to_vht_center_freq().

3) agent/src/beerocks/slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp [defensive]:
   - refresh_preference_table(): when radio->wifi_channel is empty
     (FREQ_UNKNOWN), fall back to radio->frequency_band before feeding freq_type
     into WifiChannel(chan, freq_type, bw), so the preference-table rebuild never
     constructs channels with FREQ_UNKNOWN.
   - select_next_channel(): on an empty wifi_channel use radio->frequency_band
     instead of blindly assuming FREQ_5G (which mis-attributed a 2.4GHz radio to
     5G and rejected all its channels); fall back to 5G only if the band is
     genuinely unknown.

Effect:
  - 2.4GHz radios no longer report a 5/6GHz vht_center_frequency (phy00.0
    CSA_Finished now shows the 2.4GHz center, e.g. 2437 for ch6).
  - radio->wifi_channel for a 2.4GHz radio resolves to FREQ_24G, so the
    "FREQ_TYPE: 255", "Unknown freq type 255" and "wifiChannel is empty" logs
    stop for channels 1..14.
  - The controller's dynamic_channel_selection_r2_task receives populated 2.4GHz
    operating classes / preferences instead of the "empty operating classes
    list" / empty-preference fallback.

Files changed:
  common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
  common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
  agent/src/beerocks/slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp

Notes:
  - The dynamic_channel_selection_r2_task.cpp / son_wireless_utils.cpp "255"
    messages run in the controller and are downstream EFFECTS of the agent
    emitting the wrong center/band; they are resolved by these agent-side fixes.
    No controller-side change is required.
  - build_acs_list()'s Active-ACS branch still assumes 5G on an empty
    wifi_channel (logged, active_acs_chansel_enable-only path); it can be given
    the same radio->frequency_band fallback if Active-ACS is used on 2.4GHz.

Signed-off-by: Manoj Sekar <sekar@qti.qualcomm.com>
---
 .../slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp    | 30 ++++++++++++--
 .../bwl/nl80211/ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp       | 40 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../bwl/nl80211/base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp     | 18 ++++++++-
 3 files changed, 81 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/agent/src/beerocks/slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp b/agent/src/beerocks/slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp
index 40309f6..8bb534d 100644
--- a/agent/src/beerocks/slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp
+++ b/agent/src/beerocks/slave/tasks/channel_selection_task.cpp
@@ -269,7 +269,18 @@ bool ChannelSelectionTask::refresh_preference_table(const sMacAddr &radio_mac,
         return false;
     }
 
-    const auto freq_type = radio->wifi_channel.get_freq_type();
+    auto freq_type = radio->wifi_channel.get_freq_type();
+    if (freq_type == beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_UNKNOWN) {
+        // radio->wifi_channel can be empty (FREQ_UNKNOWN) when its center frequency could not be
+        // resolved (e.g. a transient wrong/zero center freq made WifiChannel construction fall
+        // back to an empty channel). Fall back to the radio's known frequency band so downstream
+        // WifiChannel(chan, freq_type, bw) construction does not receive FREQ_UNKNOWN (which yields
+        // center_freq=0 -> are_params_valid() fails -> empty channel + the "FREQ_TYPE: 255" log).
+        freq_type = radio->frequency_band;
+        LOG(DEBUG) << "[CBS] refresh_preference_table: radio " << radio_mac
+                   << " wifi_channel is empty (FREQ_UNKNOWN); falling back to radio frequency_band "
+                   << int(freq_type);
+    }
 
     // Filter incoming ranks by this radio's frequency band.
     // In MLO (multi-link) setups, the HAL may sometimes deliver an ACS report containing
@@ -3775,9 +3786,20 @@ ChannelSelectionTask::sSelectedChannel ChannelSelectionTask::select_next_channel
     }
 
     auto freq_type = radio->wifi_channel.get_freq_type();
-    if (radio->wifi_channel.is_empty()) {
-        LOG(WARNING) << "wifi channel is empty";
-        freq_type = beerocks::FREQ_5G;
+    if (radio->wifi_channel.is_empty() || freq_type == beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_UNKNOWN) {
+        // wifi_channel can be empty (FREQ_UNKNOWN) when its center frequency could not be resolved.
+        // Prefer the radio's known frequency_band instead of blindly assuming 5G: forcing 5G on a
+        // 2.4GHz radio makes get_operating_class_by_channel()/WifiChannel() reject every 2.4GHz
+        // channel and also emits the "FREQ_TYPE: 255"/wrong-band constructions. Only fall back to
+        // 5G if the band is genuinely unknown.
+        if (radio->frequency_band != beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_UNKNOWN) {
+            freq_type = radio->frequency_band;
+            LOG(WARNING) << "wifi channel is empty; using radio frequency_band "
+                         << beerocks::utils::convert_frequency_type_to_string(freq_type);
+        } else {
+            freq_type = beerocks::FREQ_5G;
+            LOG(WARNING) << "wifi channel is empty and radio frequency_band is unknown; assuming 5G";
+        }
     } else {
         LOG(WARNING) << "freq_type: "
                      << beerocks::utils::convert_frequency_type_to_string(freq_type);
diff --git a/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp b/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
index 426a245..1cc6fbc 100644
--- a/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
+++ b/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/ap_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
@@ -6862,7 +6862,45 @@ bool ap_wlan_hal_nl80211::process_nl80211_event(parsed_obj_map_t &parsed_obj)
         m_radio_info.bandwidth =
             beerocks::utils::convert_bandwidth_to_enum(wpa_bw_to_beerocks_bw(bandwidth));
         m_radio_info.channel_ext_above  = beerocks::string_utils::stoi(parsed_obj["ch_offset"]);
-        m_radio_info.vht_center_freq    = beerocks::string_utils::stoi(parsed_obj["cf1"]);
+
+        // Band-guard the hostapd-reported center frequency (cf1). On MLO platforms the
+        // CTRL-EVENT-CHANNEL-SWITCH is relayed for every link over the shared/global hostapd
+        // socket, so a 2.4GHz radio can receive an event whose cf1 belongs to a co-located
+        // 5/6GHz link (e.g. cf1=5530 on 2.4GHz channel 6). Writing that verbatim into
+        // vht_center_freq makes fill_cs_params() report a 5GHz center on the 2.4GHz radio's
+        // CSA_Finished/ACS_Completed notification (and later makes son_slave build an empty
+        // FREQ_UNKNOWN WifiChannel). Only accept cf1 when it belongs to this radio's own band;
+        // otherwise derive the correct center from the (already band-correct) primary freq below.
+        {
+            const int cf1 = beerocks::string_utils::stoi(parsed_obj["cf1"]);
+            const int primary_freq = beerocks::string_utils::stoi(parsed_obj["freq"]);
+            const auto radio_band  = m_radio_info.frequency_band;
+            const auto cf1_band    = son::wireless_utils::which_freq_type(cf1);
+            // Normalize 5GL/5GH to 5G for the comparison.
+            auto norm_band = [](beerocks::eFreqType b) {
+                return (b == beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_5GL || b == beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_5GH)
+                           ? beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_5G
+                           : b;
+            };
+            if (cf1 > 0 &&
+                (radio_band == beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_UNKNOWN ||
+                 norm_band(cf1_band) == norm_band(radio_band))) {
+                m_radio_info.vht_center_freq = cf1;
+            } else {
+                // cf1 is cross-band (or unknown). Fall back to the primary frequency for 20MHz,
+                // which is always correct for this radio's band; wider bandwidths keep whatever
+                // refresh_radio_info() derived (band-correct) rather than a leaked cross-band cf1.
+                if (m_radio_info.bandwidth == beerocks::eWiFiBandwidth::BANDWIDTH_20 &&
+                    primary_freq > 0) {
+                    m_radio_info.vht_center_freq = primary_freq;
+                }
+                LOG(WARNING) << "CTRL_Channel_Switch: ignoring cross-band cf1=" << cf1
+                             << " (band " << int(cf1_band) << ") on radio band "
+                             << int(radio_band) << " iface=" << m_radio_info.iface_name
+                             << " -> vht_center_freq=" << m_radio_info.vht_center_freq;
+            }
+        }
+
         m_radio_info.is_dfs_channel     = beerocks::string_utils::stoi(parsed_obj["dfs"]);
         m_radio_info.last_csa_sw_reason = ChanSwReason::Unknown;
         if (son::wireless_utils::which_freq_type(m_radio_info.vht_center_freq) ==
diff --git a/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp b/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
index e842e71..49cbd10 100644
--- a/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
+++ b/common/beerocks/bwl/nl80211/base_wlan_hal_nl80211.cpp
@@ -851,9 +851,23 @@ bool base_wlan_hal_nl80211::refresh_radio_info()
                     break;
                 }
             }
-            // Always use nl80211 center frequency as it's more reliable
-            if (if_info.frequency_center1 > 0) {
+            // Always use nl80211 center frequency as it's more reliable, BUT only when it
+            // belongs to this radio's own band. On MLO platforms several links (2.4/5/6 GHz)
+            // share a single base netdev (e.g. wlan0_link0/1/2 all map to wlan0), so a
+            // GET_INTERFACE on the shared base interface can return a co-located link's
+            // center frequency (e.g. a 5 GHz 5530) and wrongly attribute it to a 2.4 GHz
+            // radio. Guard against that cross-band leak here.
+            if (if_info.frequency_center1 > 0 &&
+                (m_radio_info.frequency_band == beerocks::eFreqType::FREQ_UNKNOWN ||
+                 son::wireless_utils::which_freq_type(if_info.frequency_center1) ==
+                     m_radio_info.frequency_band)) {
                 m_radio_info.vht_center_freq = if_info.frequency_center1;
+            } else if (if_info.frequency_center1 > 0) {
+                LOG(DEBUG) << "Ignoring nl80211 center freq " << if_info.frequency_center1
+                           << " (band " << son::wireless_utils::which_freq_type(
+                                                if_info.frequency_center1)
+                           << ") - does not match radio band " << m_radio_info.frequency_band
+                           << " on iface " << m_radio_info.iface_name;
             }
         }
 
